@h1 What is Wit? h1@

Wit is a prose-first markup language for people who write — and for the
systems that consume what they write. You type plain text, and it stays plain
text. Structure, data, and logic layer in only where you reach for them.

@h2 Prose is the default h2@

Most of any document is just prose. A blank line starts a new paragraph, and
nothing needs marking up. You never fight the tool to write a sentence — the
markup gets out of the way until you want it.

@h2 Structure when you need it h2@

When you do need a heading, a table, a citation, or a reusable component, you
reach for a @em node em@ — and only there. Everything around it stays ordinary
prose, readable in a plain text editor and friendly to @strong git strong@.

@h2 Where Wit fits h2@

@table |rows [[Tool, Loved for, Breaks when you need], [Markdown, quick prose, captions, data, conditionals], [HTML, total control, it stops being writing], [LaTeX, beautiful output, the syntax and the toolchain], [Wit, prose that scales into structure, it is still young]]|

@h2 One source, many outputs h2@

The same document renders to semantic HTML, to Markdown, or to a paginated,
print-ready PDF — with a single command. Keep the source in git as text;
compile a reader-ready draft in seconds.

@blockquote Write the paper. Not the preamble. blockquote@
